  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Calculate ${\u222c}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-2,0]\\times [-1,1].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(6{x}^{2}-10{y}^{4})\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}6{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A-{\u222c}_{R}10{y}^{4}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}6{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{-2}^{0}6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{x}^{3}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-2\\cdot (-2)^{3}) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}10{y}^{4}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(0-(-2)){\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{y}^{5}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot {1}^{5}-2\\cdot (-1)^{5}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(6{x}^{2}+10{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A=32-8=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
